WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Wolves entering 29 Wisconsin counties cut dear vehicle collisions by 24% each year. This is unintended consequences, episode 149. The path runs from the recovery that brought the Wolves back, through the roads the animals started using, to the policy debates that followed, and then the lesson. In the winter of 2009, a radio-colored wolf crossed a county road in northern Wisconsin. The wolf paused and sent marked a stretch of pavement that also served as a dear travel court

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:35.400
door. Within two years, local deer began shifting their movement away from that same stretch.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.700
The change did not show up first as a howl in the woods. It showed up in the county sheriff's

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.500
thinner stack of collision reports. Jennifer Rainer was a graduate student when she started

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.220
studying wolves. State and federal agencies had spent decades trying to bring the animals

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.900
back under the Endangered Species Act. The goal was straightforward. Restore an apex predator

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.740
whose removal had let white-tailed deer numbers climb unchecked across the great lake states.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.700
Managers and biologists saw recovery as an ecological correction.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:09.300
They also hoped that correction would ease chronic deer damage to farms and forests.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.660
At the time, the most visible costs of wolves were livestock losses.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.760
Those losses were easy to tally, and the state could write a check for them.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.220
Any wider benefit states speculative.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.100
Rainer later recalled that the costs of predators are salient and simple to measure.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.420
The benefits, she recalled, often run through indirect ecological pathways that are harder

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.440
to put a number on.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.000
That is a fair description of the information managers had.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.500
A dead calf is a fact you can photograph.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.940
A deer that never steps onto a road at dusk is a fact you may never see.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.160
Wisconsin's wolves had grown from a handful of animals, recolonizing from Minnesota and

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.980
Michigan in the 1970s.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.340
By the early 2000s, the question was no longer whether wolves could persist.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.780
The question was how their presence would ripple outward into human systems.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.780
Endangered species act listings, and the delisting attempts that followed, focused almost entirely

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.760
on pack counts and livestock compensation formulas.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.240
Road safety effects sat outside that formal accounting.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.140
Nobody had to be careless for that gap to open.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.000
The ledgers were built around the harms people already knew how to count.

00:02:17.964 --> 00:02:23.224
Gray wolves recolonized Wisconsin on their own, moving in from Minnesota and Michigan starting

00:02:23.224 --> 00:02:29.004
in the mid-1970s. Growth studied after federal protection strengthened in the 1990s.

00:02:29.444 --> 00:02:34.944
By 2010, Wolves occupied 29 of the 63 counties tracked in long-term records.

00:02:35.524 --> 00:02:39.784
The state briefly delisted Wolves in 2012 and allowed limited hunting.

00:02:40.524 --> 00:02:43.224
Legal challenges brought the animals back onto the list.

00:02:43.724 --> 00:02:48.524
Through all of that, wildlife agencies kept their eyes on packed territories and on verified

00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:53.564
livestock claims. Effects on roads were not what the monitoring was built to catch.

00:02:54.144 --> 00:02:59.084
Department of Natural Resources maps recorded the year each county first held a resident pack.

00:02:59.704 --> 00:03:04.764
That record later became a natural experiment. Researchers could line the first pack year

00:03:04.764 --> 00:03:08.844
up against collision statistics and ask what changed after wolves arrived.

00:03:09.604 --> 00:03:13.624
Early monitoring emphasized minimum wolf counts and compensation payments.

00:03:13.624 --> 00:03:19.604
Those payments averaged roughly $3 million statewide over 35 years. No one

00:03:19.604 --> 00:03:24.184
at the time collected systematic before and after data on deer movement along the paved

00:03:24.184 --> 00:03:29.784
roads wolves began using as travel corridors. The animals were being counted. The pavement

00:03:29.784 --> 00:03:36.180
was not. Rainer later worked with Corbett Granger and Dominic Parker. The team matched wolf

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:43.460
maps to deer vehicle collision data from 1988 to 2010 across those 63 counties. In the

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:48.220
The county's wolves entered, collisions fell, only about 1 quarter of that drop traced

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:49.520
to fewer deer overall.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.540
The larger share came from deer changing their behavior near roads that wolves used as

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:55.280
travel corridors.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:04:00.760
The study was published in the proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, PNAS,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.080
in 2021.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.860
The average county saved roughly $375,000 a year in collision costs.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.140
Across Wisconsin, the annual total reached about $10.9 million.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:18.140
dollars. That figure was roughly 63 times the verified livestock losses the state had paid out

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:23.620
over the preceding decades. Picture the comparison this way. For every dollar the compensation

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.700
program had been writing checks to cover, the roads were quietly returning many times that

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:34.440
amount and crashes that did not happen. Drivers on those roads experienced fewer nighttime impacts.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.200
Speed limits did not change. Fencing did not change. Traffic volume did not change.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.260
the mechanism sat in how wolves move. Wolves treat roads as efficient hunting

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:50.220
paths. They travel those paths at dusk and dawn, the same hours when deer are

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:55.160
active. The result is what researchers call a landscape of fear. Dears stay

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:59.060
farther from the pavement edge even when overall deer density falls only

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:03.680
modestly. Of the reduction roughly 6 percentage points came from lower deer

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.900
numbers. The remaining 18 points came from behavioral avoidance. The researchers

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:13.900
Hunters isolated that split by comparing counties with similar deer harvest rates, but different

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:20.580
wolf occupancy. The split matters for a practical reason. Recreational hunters remove deer. Hunters

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:26.100
do not patrol the same road corridors at the same times, or with the same consistency that wolves

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:31.740
do. Hunter activity does not generate the same localized avoidance. A deer can learn that a

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:37.080
particular stretch of pavement is a bad place to linger at dusk. A deer does not learn that lesson

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.300
from a hunting season that happens somewhere else in the county, on a different clock.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.820
The data ran from 1988 to 2010.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.800
Wolfpacks established territories gradually across those years.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.700
The team could treat each county's first documented pack year as the start of the effect.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.360
No corresponding drop appeared in the 34 counties that remained wolf-free.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.800
The pattern held after the researchers controlled for changes in traffic volume and in reporting

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:04.440
practices.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:09.380
So, the $10.9 million is not a guess about deer disappearing from the landscape.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.980
It reflects fewer crashes, and the medical costs, vehicle repairs, and insurance costs

00:06:14.980 --> 00:06:17.320
that each avoided incident did not generate.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.260
That is a second order effect sitting on top of a first order one.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.320
Wolves changed where deer stood.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.620
Where deer stood changed who hit them.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.060
Who was not hit changed what counties spent on wrecks, injuries, and claims.

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:35.796
names. None of that required a new speed limit or a new mile of fence. Wildlife managers

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:40.456
in Wisconsin and in neighboring states began citing the collision data when they weighed

00:06:40.456 --> 00:06:45.836
wolf hunting seasons and delisting petitions. The finding did not reverse any policy outright.

00:06:46.456 --> 00:06:51.476
It did supply a concrete dollar figure that earlier debates had been missing. Rainer noted

00:06:51.476 --> 00:06:56.356
that recreational hunting removes deer, yet does not recreate the same landscape of fear

00:06:56.356 --> 00:07:02.776
along roads. Hunters do not patrol the linear corridors Wolves' favor. Similar analyses are now

00:07:02.776 --> 00:07:09.276
underway in other states where Wolfrange overlaps dense road networks. The PNAS paper itself became

00:07:09.276 --> 00:07:14.776
part of public comment records during later delisting discussions. Agencies had a quantified

00:07:14.776 --> 00:07:24.156
co-benefit to set beside livestock compensation totals. No new fencing program was altered as a

00:07:24.156 --> 00:07:29.116
director's ult, either. What shifted was the frame of the argument. The debate had lived

00:07:29.116 --> 00:07:34.736
inside ecology and agriculture. It now had to make room for measurable returns on road safety.

00:07:35.396 --> 00:07:40.296
The original managers had not been wrong to compensate ranchers. Those losses were real,

00:07:40.556 --> 00:07:45.136
and they were the costs the system knew how to see. The study did not erase that ledger.

00:07:45.556 --> 00:07:50.942
It added a column the listings and delistings had left blank. When Rainer's team compared

00:07:50.942 --> 00:07:55.542
counties that gained wolves with counties that did not, the largest safety gain showed

00:07:55.542 --> 00:08:00.462
up where wolves used roads as hunting paths. It did not show up simply where total deer

00:08:00.462 --> 00:08:05.622
numbers fell. That pattern is the mechanism worth keeping. A single management choice,

00:08:05.922 --> 00:08:10.782
maintaining wolf presence in a county, can move prey at specific places without a separate

00:08:10.782 --> 00:08:16.062
road safety budget. The same data set showed that the benefit scaled with wolf occupancy,

00:08:16.062 --> 00:08:18.102
Not with hunter harvest rates.

00:08:18.602 --> 00:08:23.242
Managers cannot assume that human hunting stands in for every effect a predator produces.

00:08:23.902 --> 00:08:28.262
States now weighing wolf policy can put their own collision numbers and their own livestock

00:08:28.262 --> 00:08:33.442
compensation numbers into the Wisconsin framework and ask whether the same ratio holds.

00:08:33.962 --> 00:08:38.542
The study is a template for indirect benefits that appear only after a predator has changed

00:08:38.542 --> 00:08:41.422
fine-scale prey behavior along human infrastructure.

00:08:41.422 --> 00:08:47.122
If you follow how agencies count what a recovered animal is worth, environmental intelligence

00:08:47.122 --> 00:08:50.842
is the narrow show that stays with those ledgers after the headline fades.

00:08:51.402 --> 00:08:55.902
When your state next weighs a wolf season or a delisting, what would you watch for in

00:08:55.902 --> 00:08:56.442
the spreadsheet?

00:08:57.042 --> 00:09:00.682
Only the livestock checks or the dusk roads where the fear actually lives?

00:09:01.302 --> 00:09:02.422
That's the case for today.

00:09:03.062 --> 00:09:07.542
The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd

00:09:07.542 --> 00:09:10.342
find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

